    <slider x="1900" step="0.05" y="302" max="1" width="260" display="hgt-transfer-prob" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.5" variable="hgt-transfer-prob"></slider>
    <slider x="1900" step="0.001" y="360" max="0.05" width="260" display="persister-switch-prob" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.001" variable="persister-switch-prob"></slider>
    <slider x="1900" step="0.005" y="418" max="0.1" width="260" display="persister-wake-prob" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.05" variable="persister-wake-prob"></slider>
    <slider x="1900" step="1" y="476" max="10" width="260" display="max-cells-per-patch" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="8.0" variable="max-cells-per-patch"></slider>
    <slider x="1900" step="0.05" y="534" max="1" width="260" display="nutrient-diffusion" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.6" variable="nutrient-diffusion"></slider>
    <slider x="1900" step="0.01" y="592" max="1" width="260" display="wellmixed-level" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="0.08" variable="wellmixed-level"></slider>
    <slider x="1900" step="0.05" y="650" max="1" width="260" display="source-concentration" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="source-concentration"></slider>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

An agent-based model of bacterial biofilm growth under antibiotic pressure. Every bacterium is an autonomous agent with its own metabolic energy, resistance level and individual MIC/MBC thresholds. The environment is a 2-D grid with nutrients replenished from the substrate (bottom) and antibiotic entering from the top, diffusing downward and decaying. The model is used to study how spatial structure protects the population and how resistance evolves through mutation and horizontal gene transfer (HGT).

The model accompanies the Diploma Thesis of Athanasios Nakos and Vasileios Galanis (supervisor: Aspasia Daskalopoulou), Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Thessaly, 2026. All quantities are dimensionless; time is measured in ticks and distance in patches.

## HOW IT WORKS

Each tick: (1) antibiotic enters from the top band, diffuses and decays; (2) every live cell may switch into or out of the dormant (persister) state, moves slightly, takes up nutrient scaled by a growth factor (1 below MIC, falling linearly to 0 at MBC) and by the fitness cost of resistance, deposits resistance genes into the EPS if its resistance exceeds `Triggerhgt` (only after biofilm formation), takes up EPS genes that exceed its own resistance, may be killed if the local antibiotic exceeds its MBC, divides when its energy exceeds the threshold (with a chance of mutation), and pays a metabolic cost; (3) nutrients are replenished at the substrate and optionally diffuse upward; (4) EPS genes diffuse and decay; (5) structural metrics are computed.

Biofilm formation (quorum sensing) is a population-level state that switches on once the live population exceeds `quorum-threshold`. Dead cells stay on the grid in gray. When no live cell exists, the reporters emit -999 as a sentinel value.

## HOW TO USE IT

Press **setup**, then **go**.

Core parameters: `initial-number-bacteria`, `spawncellposition` (height of the initial band), `nutrientdensity`, `idealenvironemt-abovesubstrate` (thickness of the substrate band), `quorum-threshold`, `reproduction-energy-threshold`, `metabolic-rate`, `mutation-chance`, `mbc-mic-ratio`.

Antibiotic: `source-concentration`, `antibioticapplication-belowtop` (thickness of the source band), `antibiotic-diffusion`, `antibiotic-decay` (1 = no decay).

HGT: `hgt-enabled?`, `Triggerhgt` (resistance above which a cell releases genes), `hgt-transfer-prob`, `resistancegenesateps-diffuse-rate`, `resistancegenesateps-natural-decay`.

Extended mechanisms (set to 0 to disable): `resistance-cost`, `persister-switch-prob` / `persister-wake-prob`, `max-cells-per-patch` (volume exclusion), `nutrient-diffusion`.

Scenario switches: `strong-penetration?` forces the drug to reach the biofilm base; `well-mixed?` holds every patch at `wellmixed-level` (dose-matched control without spatial gradient); `verbose?` enables pop-up messages (keep off for BehaviorSpace).

Plots show live/dead population, mean resistance, mean environmental antibiotic, mutation count and the smoothed shape factor. Colours: blue = initial cells, yellow-to-orange = descendants (hue by resistance), gray = dead; patches are green (nutrient) or red (antibiotic).

## THINGS TO NOTICE

In the baseline regime the antibiotic decays before it reaches the biofilm, so the population is unaffected even at high dose. Switch on `strong-penetration?` and the population collapses while resistance rises through selection. With `resistance-cost` = 0 and HGT enabled, resistance fixes abruptly below a critical `Triggerhgt`; with a cost it no longer does.

## THINGS TO TRY

Compare `max-cells-per-patch` and `nutrient-diffusion` separately and together: vertical structure (bf-height, bf-spread) emerges only when both are active. Use the BehaviorSpace experiments (Tools > BehaviorSpace) to reproduce the experiments of the thesis; the analysis script in the repository produces the figures and statistics.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Source code, BehaviorSpace experiments, analysis script and data: https://github.com/USERNAME/biofilm-abm-netlogo (MIT License). Please cite the thesis if you use this model.]]></info>
